  "title": "IMSAI 8080",
  "year": "1976",
  "subtitle": "A computer you program one toggle switch at a time — the front panel is the entire interface",
  "why_scout_cares": "The IMSAI 8080 is the purest front-panel machine of the early microcomputer era and the museum has nothing at the bit-level interface: no OS, no monitor, no keyboard. The human is the boot loader, the monitor, and the assembler. Its manual even ships a game played by flipping the front-panel sense switches and watching the programmed-output LEDs — the panel doubles as a game controller. A completely new interaction family for the collection.",
